<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Llywelyn the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1173–<a href="/years/1240">1240</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Llywelyn ap Iorwerth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Llywelyn the Great, Llywelyn ap Iorwerth, was prince of Gwynedd who made himself the dominant ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. He married Joan, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdyfi">Aberdyfi</a> in 1216 the other Welsh rulers accepted his overlordship. He built stone castles, issued charters and secured his power through diplomacy and war. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a> later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/llywelyn-the-great.jpg" alt="Llywelyn the Great" width="320" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tipcake · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pen_Llywelyn_ab_Iorwerth,_Castell_Degannwy.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
